<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # My Family and I – Lesson # 3</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;"><i>Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</i></p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 20px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 3</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Speaking</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 1st Grade (CEFR: Pre-A1.3)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> My Family and I</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> She Is My Mom!</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Speaking: Productive: Locate objects and people using words. Interactive: Respond nonverbally to simple yes/no questions by nodding or shaking head.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Speaking: Can identify family members, using simple sentences (e.g., "She is my mom."). Can answer basic yes/no questions about family members, using nods, gestures, or simple words (e.g., "Is she your mom?"). Can participate in conversations about their family, using one-word responses (e.g., "Who is she?" "Mom.") or two-word responses (e.g., "Who is she?" "My sister.").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 35%; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 15%; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher shows large, colorful flashcards of family members (mom, dad, brother, sister, grandmother, grandfather). The teacher holds up a flashcard and says the name (e.g., "Mom!"). Students repeat chorally. The teacher leads a simple TPR activity: "Point to mom!" "Point to dad!" Students physically point to the correct flashcard or to themselves if applicable for "my mom."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models introducing family members using simple sentences. Holding up a flashcard of a mom, the teacher says clearly: "She is my mom." (emphasizing /m/ sound). Holding up a dad flashcard, the teacher says: "He is my dad." (emphasizing /d/ sound). The teacher uses gestures, pointing to the flashcard and then to themselves to imply "my." Students imitate the full sentence chorally after the teacher.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding. The teacher holds up a flashcard of a brother and asks, "Is she my mom?" Students shake their heads and say "No." The teacher then holds up a flashcard of a mom and asks, "Is she my mom?" Students nod their heads and say "Yes." The teacher might also ask, "Is she a boy?" when showing a girl, waiting for a "No" response to clarify gender pronouns.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "My Family and I".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A large, colorful family poster (showing mom, dad, brother, sister, grandmother, grandfather, possibly a pet) or a short, illustrated picture story featuring simple dialogues about family members.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Introducing family members using simple sentences (e.g., "She is my mom.") and responding to basic questions about family members (e.g., "Who is he?" "He is my brother.").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher points to different family members on the poster and asks, "Who is she?" or "Who is he?" Students respond with one-word answers (e.g., "Mom!", "Brother!").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher asks yes/no questions about the family members on the poster, prompting nonverbal responses. "Is she a girl?" (Students nod). "Is he a sister?" (Students shake head).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher models complete sentences: "She is my mom." "He is my brother." Students point to the corresponding picture and repeat after the teacher chorally and individually.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Speaking.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Personal pronouns + verb ("She is", "He is"), Possessive adjectives ("my mom", "my brother"), WH-questions ("Who is she?", "Who is he?"). Vocabulary: mom, dad, brother, sister, grandmother, grandfather. Pronunciation: /m/, /d/, /b/, /s/ sounds.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice (Choral Repetition & Identification):</b> The teacher shows a series of family member flashcards one by one. For each card, the teacher says: "She is my mom." or "He is my dad." Students repeat chorally. Then, the teacher holds up a flashcard and asks a specific student: "Who is she/he?" The student answers with the correct pronoun and family member (e.g., "She is mom."). The teacher ensures correct pronunciation of /m/, /d/, /b/ in "brother" and /s/ in "sister."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Practice (Circle Game):</b> Students sit in a circle. The teacher passes a "magic microphone" (or a toy). When a student holds the microphone, the teacher shows a family flashcard and asks, "Who is she?" or "Who is he?" The student responds with a short answer (e.g., "Mom!", "My brother!"). If they need help, the teacher provides a scaffolded prompt like "She is..." The activity continues with different family members.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction (Flashcard Exchange):</b> Students work in pairs. Each pair receives a set of small family member flashcards. Student A picks a card, shows it to Student B, and asks, "Who is she?" or "Who is he?" Student B responds with "She is my sister." or "He is my brother." (using the possessive adjective). They then swap roles. The teacher circulates, offering support and correcting pronunciation as needed.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15-18 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "My Family and I".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> "My Family Show and Tell." Students bring a small family photo (pre-arranged by teacher instruction, or provided with simple pre-drawn family cutouts/stick figures if photos are not feasible) that they can point to.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students sit in small groups (3-4 students). Each student takes a turn to present one or two family members from their photo/drawing.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">They say: "This is my mom. She is nice." or "This is my brother. He is tall." (using adjectives as appropriate, and focusing on "my" and "She is/He is").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Other students in the group can ask simple "Who is she/he?" questions, and the presenter responds with one or two words (e.g., "Mom!", "My sister.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher moves between groups, facilitating conversation, encouraging participation, and providing support for vocabulary or sentence structure as needed. The focus is on fluency and communicative interaction rather than perfect accuracy.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist for oral production.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> As students participate in the "My Family Show and Tell" (Stage 4), the teacher observes their speaking ability and interaction in small groups.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Intelligible pronunciation of target words (mom, dad, brother, sister) and sounds (/m/, /d/, /b/, /s/).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Use of target vocabulary (mom, dad, brother, sister, grandmother, grandfather).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Correct use of "She is my..." and "He is my..." for identifying family members.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Ability to respond to "Who is she/he?" with one-word (e.g., "Mom.") or two-word (e.g., "My sister.") answers or nonverbally (nod/shake head for yes/no).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Participation and collaborative interaction with peers.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students show a "thumbs up" if they can say "She is my mom!" and "He is my dad!" (or a "thumbs down" if they need more practice). They can also point to a happy face or a neutral face drawing on a worksheet to indicate how easy/difficult it was to talk about their family.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What new family words did you practice saying today?" (e.g., "Mom!", "Brother!").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was fun about talking about your family with your friends?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What speaking activities worked best for engaging 1st graders and promoting oral production of family vocabulary and simple sentences? (e.g., TPR, flashcard games, pair work).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Which students struggled with pronunciation or sentence structure, and what adjustments or scaffolds can be provided for future speaking practice? (e.g., more choral repetition, simpler prompts, individual modeling).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">How well did students use the personal pronouns "she" and "he" and the possessive adjective "my"?</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> In the next lesson (Lesson 4: Writing Composition), students will use the family vocabulary and the sentence structures ("She is my mom," "He is my brother") they practiced speaking today to construct simple written sentences about their families.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>